WEBVTT

00:00:21.200 --> 00:00:23.000
I'm E. F. Lavert Miller.

00:00:23.000 --> 00:00:25.360
Welcome to The Scholars, a television interview series

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.600
that explores contemporary scholarship.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.240
We hope to engage and challenge your thinking

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:30.960
on a broad range of topics.

00:00:30.960 --> 00:00:32.440
You'll meet a diverse group of scholars

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:34.680
who are investigating and thinking about a complex

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:35.880
and changing world.

00:00:35.880 --> 00:00:39.040
John Carriacou is the author of The Reluctant Spy,

00:00:39.040 --> 00:00:41.880
My Secret Life in the CIA's War on Terror.

00:00:41.880 --> 00:00:43.680
He worked at the Central Intelligence Agency

00:00:43.680 --> 00:00:45.920
from 1990 to 2004.

00:00:45.920 --> 00:00:47.760
He's currently an associate fellow

00:00:47.760 --> 00:00:49.360
with the Institute for Policy Studies.

00:00:49.360 --> 00:00:50.320
Welcome to The Scholars, John.

00:00:50.320 --> 00:00:51.800
Thanks very much for having me.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:53.840
You know, I want to go back to what you described

00:00:53.840 --> 00:00:55.360
in your book, Reluctant Spy,

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.000
as a key moment in terms of the change of life.

00:00:58.000 --> 00:00:59.960
November 4th, 1979.

00:00:59.960 --> 00:01:01.840
What happened on that day?

00:01:01.840 --> 00:01:04.120
That was the day that Iranian students

00:01:04.120 --> 00:01:06.120
overtook the American Embassy

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:11.200
and took dozens of our diplomats hostage.

00:01:11.200 --> 00:01:14.160
I never even really thought much about the Middle East.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:16.160
I was 15 years old when it happened.

00:01:16.160 --> 00:01:18.920
I thought I wanted to be a dentist when I grew up.

00:01:18.920 --> 00:01:22.280
But the hostage taking in Iran

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:24.960
opened up this whole new world for me in the Middle East,

00:01:24.960 --> 00:01:27.720
and I developed a fascination with the Middle East

00:01:27.720 --> 00:01:29.160
that I carried through college.

00:01:29.160 --> 00:01:30.920
I got a degree in Middle Eastern studies,

00:01:30.920 --> 00:01:34.320
and it really did change the course of my life.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:37.360
You know, when a young person is coming out of college,

00:01:37.360 --> 00:01:40.040
they went, okay, what's next, okay?

00:01:40.040 --> 00:01:42.160
Right.

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:43.480
You've written a book about,

00:01:43.480 --> 00:01:45.600
One Never Leaves a CIA, okay?

00:01:45.600 --> 00:01:48.360
Why did you join the CIA?

00:01:48.360 --> 00:01:51.800
My family is relatively new in the United States.

00:01:51.800 --> 00:01:54.360
My father's parents came here in 1931,

00:01:54.360 --> 00:01:56.800
and my mother's parents in 1934.

00:01:56.800 --> 00:02:00.200
And my grandparents always instilled in us the idea

00:02:00.200 --> 00:02:03.320
that we were so lucky to be in this country.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:05.480
We were so fortunate for the opportunities

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.400
that we had been given for work, for education,

00:02:09.400 --> 00:02:14.000
most of all, that we needed to give back to the country.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.760
And so public service was always a big deal in our house

00:02:17.760 --> 00:02:21.080
from volunteering when I was young

00:02:21.080 --> 00:02:25.400
to going into the public sector as an adult.

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:27.440
It was always strongly encouraged.

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.520
Now, you develop skills

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:31.720
that I guess people would probably admire.

00:02:31.720 --> 00:02:32.560
You become an analyst.

00:02:32.560 --> 00:02:34.680
I mean, what does that mean, and what did you do?

00:02:34.680 --> 00:02:36.600
That was the best job in the world.

00:02:36.600 --> 00:02:38.520
It's the best job I've ever had.

00:02:38.520 --> 00:02:40.960
And in fact, even after I left the CIA,

00:02:40.960 --> 00:02:43.560
I've compared every subsequent job to that one,

00:02:43.560 --> 00:02:45.280
and none of them compare.

00:02:45.840 --> 00:02:50.840
To put it succinctly, you think the big thoughts.

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:56.240
You go onto your computer every morning when you get to work

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:59.280
and you start reading cable traffic.

00:02:59.280 --> 00:03:01.560
This is reports from the State Department.

00:03:01.560 --> 00:03:02.520
And you're a news junkie.

00:03:02.520 --> 00:03:04.040
I'm a news junkie.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:07.200
And this was the real inside story.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.920
So you get State Department reports,

00:03:08.920 --> 00:03:13.320
you get CIA reports, NSA, lots and lots of press reports.

00:03:13.360 --> 00:03:16.840
Sometimes cables from the Defense Department or the FBI.

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:19.440
And you get to see what happened overnight

00:03:19.440 --> 00:03:21.920
in the part of the world that you're covering.

00:03:21.920 --> 00:03:25.160
And once you get all this information,

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:29.280
you use your own intelligence to put it all together

00:03:29.280 --> 00:03:32.680
to write it in a cogent and tight form

00:03:32.680 --> 00:03:34.840
that the CIA teaches you.

00:03:34.840 --> 00:03:37.120
And you send those papers to the President,

00:03:37.120 --> 00:03:40.200
the Vice President, the Secretaries of State and Defense

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:41.800
and the National Security Advisor.

00:03:42.000 --> 00:03:45.160
And when you're 23, 24, 25 years old,

00:03:45.160 --> 00:03:48.760
there's nothing headier than sending a paper to the White House

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:50.720
and then having it come back to you

00:03:50.720 --> 00:03:52.680
where the President has scribbled comments

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:53.520
in the market.

00:03:53.520 --> 00:03:55.200
Let me scribble some comments right here.

00:03:55.200 --> 00:03:57.200
So you're taking all this information

00:03:57.200 --> 00:03:58.480
from here and there and there.

00:03:58.480 --> 00:04:02.000
My question now is, what is your knowledge of history?

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:03.720
You know, that's really a key.

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:06.000
And that's why an analytic job at the CIA

00:04:06.000 --> 00:04:07.240
is not for everybody.

00:04:07.240 --> 00:04:10.080
Because you really have to be a student of history.

00:04:10.120 --> 00:04:12.640
You know the old saying that those who don't know history

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:13.880
are doomed to repeat it.

00:04:13.880 --> 00:04:18.120
It's your job to make sure that the policymaker doesn't repeat it.

00:04:18.120 --> 00:04:22.240
And so you really have to be a subject matter expert.

00:04:22.240 --> 00:04:24.200
Let me mention this because I've seen some books

00:04:24.200 --> 00:04:26.000
that have come out about the CIA.

00:04:26.000 --> 00:04:29.400
And what happens is sometime in terms of a staff person

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:31.120
working one part of the world has moved over

00:04:31.120 --> 00:04:34.200
to another part of the world and might be making policy

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:36.160
with no knowledge of the history of a particular country.

00:04:36.160 --> 00:04:37.360
Is that still happening?

00:04:37.400 --> 00:04:40.720
That happens every day in our government.

00:04:40.720 --> 00:04:43.520
See, this is a problem not specific to the CIA

00:04:43.520 --> 00:04:47.240
but really all over our foreign policy

00:04:47.240 --> 00:04:50.280
and national security policy apparatus.

00:04:50.280 --> 00:04:52.600
You can be the world's greatest expert

00:04:52.600 --> 00:04:54.760
on a given topic in the CIA.

00:04:54.760 --> 00:04:59.520
But an elected official who's tasked with making policy

00:04:59.520 --> 00:05:02.720
can choose to just ignore whatever it is you have to say.

00:05:02.720 --> 00:05:05.520
Don't forget, a lot of our politicians think

00:05:05.520 --> 00:05:07.960
that they're always the smartest people in the room.

00:05:07.960 --> 00:05:11.160
And most of the time, frankly, they're not.

00:05:11.160 --> 00:05:12.120
Right.

00:05:12.120 --> 00:05:13.600
It's the person who lost an election.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:14.760
That's right.

00:05:14.760 --> 00:05:17.560
They're the most arrogant people in the room.

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:20.960
And oftentimes, they're not willing to take advice.

00:05:20.960 --> 00:05:22.320
And the advice, at least that's

00:05:22.320 --> 00:05:25.080
coming from the CIA's analytic unit,

00:05:25.080 --> 00:05:26.880
the Directorate of Intelligence,

00:05:26.880 --> 00:05:30.240
is very well thought out and coordinated advice.

00:05:30.240 --> 00:05:31.760
Let's talk about another discipline.

00:05:31.760 --> 00:05:33.480
So I mentioned history.

00:05:33.480 --> 00:05:35.600
What about being an analyst at CIA?

00:05:35.600 --> 00:05:37.760
Where does philosophy and ethics come into play,

00:05:37.760 --> 00:05:38.680
in terms of policy?

00:05:38.680 --> 00:05:40.560
That's a great question.

00:05:40.560 --> 00:05:43.840
This was a problem that I had with the CIA

00:05:43.840 --> 00:05:46.000
from the very beginning.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:48.480
Ethics is not taught at the CIA.

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:49.880
It's not stressed.

00:05:49.880 --> 00:05:52.040
It's not even touched upon.

00:05:52.040 --> 00:05:54.360
And you really have to fall back

00:05:54.360 --> 00:05:58.120
on your own personal ethics, your own personal morality,

00:05:58.120 --> 00:06:00.200
to guide the course of your career.

00:06:00.200 --> 00:06:02.760
That's less important on the analytic side

00:06:02.800 --> 00:06:04.600
than it is on the operational side.

00:06:04.600 --> 00:06:08.280
But this is something that's very sorely lacking in the CIA.

00:06:08.280 --> 00:06:11.000
You know, when we look at the CIA,

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:14.320
and we talked a minute ago about

00:06:14.320 --> 00:06:17.560
how your life was changed back in 79,

00:06:17.560 --> 00:06:20.280
what happens to the CIA after 9-11?

00:06:20.280 --> 00:06:21.920
Oh boy.

00:06:21.920 --> 00:06:25.760
Absolutely everything changed after 9-11.

00:06:25.760 --> 00:06:30.600
I had switched from analysis to operations in 1998.

00:06:30.600 --> 00:06:32.440
So I was already in operations

00:06:32.440 --> 00:06:34.880
when the September 11th attacks happened.

00:06:34.880 --> 00:06:36.360
But, you know, Ethel Burke,

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:40.200
it's as though we lost our way after September 11th.

00:06:40.200 --> 00:06:43.960
We were so obsessed with capturing or killing

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:48.960
Osama bin Laden and with disrupting a future attack

00:06:49.480 --> 00:06:52.840
that we threw whatever ethics,

00:06:52.840 --> 00:06:55.400
whatever morality we had out the window.

00:06:55.400 --> 00:06:58.300
We instituted a torture program, for example.

00:06:58.660 --> 00:07:03.300
As a government, we developed a drone program

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.220
that's used to assassinate people around the world.

00:07:06.220 --> 00:07:09.660
These are things that we wouldn't have dreamt of doing

00:07:09.660 --> 00:07:13.620
pre 9-11, but the rules all seemed to change.

00:07:13.620 --> 00:07:15.860
Well, you know, when people say that, okay,

00:07:15.860 --> 00:07:18.260
I go back to the speech that George Bush

00:07:18.260 --> 00:07:20.900
gave to Congress after 9-11.

00:07:20.900 --> 00:07:22.820
And it seems in that speech,

00:07:22.820 --> 00:07:26.060
you get this sort of mandate of the world has changed

00:07:26.100 --> 00:07:29.140
and now we will sit down and work with people

00:07:29.140 --> 00:07:31.700
that we may not wanna work with, but the world has changed.

00:07:31.700 --> 00:07:34.380
And we see this as if we become offensive

00:07:34.380 --> 00:07:36.300
in our foreign policy.

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:39.820
We've certainly become police in our foreign policy.

00:07:39.820 --> 00:07:41.260
We've offended allies.

00:07:41.260 --> 00:07:44.020
You'll recall during the Bush administration,

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:47.020
the talk, I think it was from Secretary of Defense

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:50.500
Rumsfeld about old Europe versus new Europe

00:07:50.500 --> 00:07:52.940
and that the new Europeans were with us

00:07:52.940 --> 00:07:56.300
and the old Europeans could lump it if they didn't like it.

00:07:57.660 --> 00:08:02.660
We became increasingly comfortable in a unipolar world

00:08:03.460 --> 00:08:07.900
which I think was wrong headed

00:08:07.900 --> 00:08:12.220
and I think offended other countries

00:08:12.220 --> 00:08:15.660
that might have been willing to work with us otherwise.

00:08:15.660 --> 00:08:18.900
And this whole thing,

00:08:18.900 --> 00:08:22.920
our entire national security structure changed

00:08:22.920 --> 00:08:24.280
to the point where,

00:08:25.320 --> 00:08:27.200
well really for most of the Bush administration,

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:29.880
I had never seen an administration that worked so hard

00:08:29.880 --> 00:08:33.400
to not use diplomacy as the Bush administration.

00:08:33.400 --> 00:08:37.080
I was very confident when President Obama was elected

00:08:37.080 --> 00:08:38.640
because I thought finally,

00:08:38.640 --> 00:08:42.000
we're gonna have some realpolitik

00:08:42.000 --> 00:08:43.920
and we're gonna have people who think about this kind

00:08:43.920 --> 00:08:46.040
of thing and people who use diplomacy,

00:08:46.040 --> 00:08:47.560
we're gonna have peace talks.

00:08:47.560 --> 00:08:51.360
Let's go back to Jimi Carter's administration.

00:08:51.360 --> 00:08:52.640
Well you might recall,

00:08:52.640 --> 00:08:54.460
human rights became very important.

00:08:54.460 --> 00:08:56.720
In fact, that's when the State Department started writing

00:08:56.720 --> 00:08:58.920
at the annual Human Rights Report was under President Carter.

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:01.600
And some people felt that Carter was a weak president.

00:09:01.600 --> 00:09:04.120
And some people felt that he was compromising

00:09:04.120 --> 00:09:06.680
in areas that he should not have compromised,

00:09:06.680 --> 00:09:08.560
Nicaragua being one.

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:10.120
How do we look at that now?

00:09:11.440 --> 00:09:13.160
I think that historians will tell you

00:09:13.160 --> 00:09:14.920
that Carter was right.

00:09:14.920 --> 00:09:16.440
I think that historians will say

00:09:16.440 --> 00:09:19.840
that we grossly underestimated Jimmy Carter as president.

00:09:20.800 --> 00:09:23.040
The fact that he's the best former president

00:09:23.040 --> 00:09:24.480
we've ever had aside,

00:09:26.080 --> 00:09:29.880
he guided very difficult relations with Pakistan

00:09:29.880 --> 00:09:32.680
from 1977 to 1981.

00:09:33.760 --> 00:09:37.380
This was a time when the Soviets had invaded Afghanistan

00:09:37.380 --> 00:09:40.720
and we were really reliant on the Pakistani government

00:09:40.720 --> 00:09:45.360
at a time of its own turmoil to help us craft the policy.

00:09:45.360 --> 00:09:48.320
It was Jimmy Carter that led that policy.

00:09:48.320 --> 00:09:51.280
We could easily have gone to war with Iran

00:09:51.280 --> 00:09:53.200
after the hostage crisis.

00:09:53.200 --> 00:09:57.240
And while Carter tried to rescue the hostages,

00:09:57.240 --> 00:09:58.640
it failed of course.

00:09:58.640 --> 00:10:01.560
But we could easily have invaded that country

00:10:01.560 --> 00:10:03.200
to try to win the freedom of our hostages

00:10:03.200 --> 00:10:05.720
or more importantly to punish the Iranian government

00:10:05.720 --> 00:10:07.080
for taking them in the first place.

00:10:07.080 --> 00:10:08.000
And we didn't.

00:10:08.000 --> 00:10:09.280
Well let's look at the big issue here

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.440
that we might see in terms of the change.

00:10:11.440 --> 00:10:14.000
And that is we're moving from a cold war mentality

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:15.880
into this new war against terrorism.

00:10:15.880 --> 00:10:17.520
How does the battle against communism

00:10:17.520 --> 00:10:20.120
now compare to the battle against terrorism?

00:10:20.120 --> 00:10:22.600
Oh the battle against communism was much, much easier

00:10:22.600 --> 00:10:23.440
for two reasons.

00:10:23.440 --> 00:10:26.080
First of all, you knew who your enemy was.

00:10:26.080 --> 00:10:28.300
And secondly, you knew that you could sit across

00:10:28.300 --> 00:10:30.360
the table from your enemy and negotiate

00:10:30.360 --> 00:10:32.560
no matter how difficult the issue was.

00:10:32.560 --> 00:10:34.540
And that's not the world we live in today.

00:10:34.540 --> 00:10:36.040
What is the world we live in today?

00:10:36.040 --> 00:10:39.120
The world we live in today is that we refuse

00:10:39.120 --> 00:10:42.680
to negotiate even if the other side wants to.

00:10:42.680 --> 00:10:44.400
It's our policy that we don't.

00:10:44.400 --> 00:10:49.400
We are much more comfortable using armed drones.

00:10:50.200 --> 00:10:54.160
And we're more comfortable using combat troops

00:10:54.160 --> 00:10:56.400
to express our foreign policy.

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.160
You mentioned Runsville previously.

00:10:58.160 --> 00:11:01.200
I remember Runsville made a statement a number of years ago

00:11:01.200 --> 00:11:04.080
that there would be no need to place a US soldier

00:11:04.080 --> 00:11:05.560
in harm's way because of the emphasis

00:11:05.560 --> 00:11:07.520
on robotic in terms of drones.

00:11:07.520 --> 00:11:10.280
And it seems as if we've passed that point.

00:11:10.320 --> 00:11:14.480
We've passed it to the point where we're murdering

00:11:14.480 --> 00:11:16.840
even American citizens overseas.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:20.240
American citizens who have not been charged with a crime.

00:11:20.240 --> 00:11:22.240
American citizens who have not had the right

00:11:22.240 --> 00:11:24.720
to confront their accusers in a court of law.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:27.660
And in at least two cases,

00:11:27.660 --> 00:11:30.280
American citizens who were wrongly targeted.

00:11:30.280 --> 00:11:31.960
And then we don't even issue so much

00:11:31.960 --> 00:11:33.960
as an apology for those attacks.

00:11:33.960 --> 00:11:35.480
Let's talk about some definitions here.

00:11:35.480 --> 00:11:38.080
We hear often the word terrorism being used.

00:11:38.080 --> 00:11:39.880
How is that defined?

00:11:39.880 --> 00:11:43.120
I would define terrorism as any act meant

00:11:43.120 --> 00:11:46.560
to instill terror in a broader audience.

00:11:46.560 --> 00:11:49.560
And I think that both the Bush and Obama administrations

00:11:49.560 --> 00:11:51.360
have defined it differently.

00:11:51.360 --> 00:11:56.360
They would define it as any act in and of itself

00:11:56.760 --> 00:12:00.280
that poses a threat to the security of the United States.

00:12:00.280 --> 00:12:01.120
Period.

00:12:01.120 --> 00:12:05.000
I was reading your book and I got on page 135 and 136.

00:12:05.000 --> 00:12:07.520
You outlined some of the techniques.

00:12:07.520 --> 00:12:09.520
I couldn't even get through reading some of them.

00:12:10.160 --> 00:12:12.440
And seeing this if, for example,

00:12:12.440 --> 00:12:14.480
let it for the definition that you just gave up with,

00:12:14.480 --> 00:12:16.480
I wouldn't want to subject anybody to this.

00:12:16.480 --> 00:12:17.600
No.

00:12:17.600 --> 00:12:20.120
No, what you're talking about is the torture techniques

00:12:20.120 --> 00:12:21.840
that we use to get prisoners to.

00:12:21.840 --> 00:12:23.160
I mentioned like two or three of them.

00:12:23.160 --> 00:12:27.200
There are three that are really, I think horrific.

00:12:27.200 --> 00:12:31.240
The one that's garnered the most press is waterboarding.

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:35.320
Where a person is laid on a gurney

00:12:35.320 --> 00:12:37.520
with the feet slightly elevated.

00:12:37.520 --> 00:12:40.680
His face is covered with some sort of material burlap,

00:12:40.680 --> 00:12:41.760
cloth, whatever.

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:43.800
And then water is poured on his face.

00:12:43.800 --> 00:12:46.200
It instills a sense of drowning,

00:12:47.480 --> 00:12:50.100
which is very painful because your body reacts to it.

00:12:50.100 --> 00:12:53.980
Your body contorts, your abdominal muscles tense up

00:12:53.980 --> 00:12:56.400
and you really do feel like you're drowning.

00:12:56.400 --> 00:12:57.840
But there are a couple of others

00:12:57.840 --> 00:13:01.000
that I always thought were worse than waterboarding.

00:13:01.000 --> 00:13:03.920
One is what's called the cold cell,

00:13:03.920 --> 00:13:08.920
where a prison cell is chilled to about 50 degrees.

00:13:08.920 --> 00:13:10.520
A prisoner is stripped naked.

00:13:10.520 --> 00:13:12.080
He's thrown in the cell

00:13:12.080 --> 00:13:15.680
and then ice water is thrown on him every hour or so.

00:13:15.680 --> 00:13:17.400
We had a prisoner in Afghanistan

00:13:17.400 --> 00:13:19.080
by the name of Hassan Gul

00:13:19.080 --> 00:13:21.240
who did not survive that technique

00:13:21.240 --> 00:13:24.720
and died during the night because of the cold.

00:13:24.720 --> 00:13:27.880
There's a third one and that is sleep deprivation.

00:13:27.880 --> 00:13:30.340
And I don't mean keeping a prisoner awake

00:13:30.340 --> 00:13:32.600
for a night, two nights, even three nights.

00:13:32.600 --> 00:13:34.880
I'm talking about keeping a prisoner awake

00:13:34.880 --> 00:13:36.920
for two weeks at a time

00:13:36.920 --> 00:13:39.560
where they're chained to an eye bolt in the ceiling

00:13:39.560 --> 00:13:41.100
so they can't lay down,

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:44.620
they can't really even kneel down or crouch down,

00:13:44.620 --> 00:13:46.760
and they can't hang from the eye bolt

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.600
because it's so painful after a period of time.

00:13:49.600 --> 00:13:51.440
We want to describe, I remember this goes back

00:13:51.440 --> 00:13:54.200
to Norieger and they were playing music constantly.

00:13:54.200 --> 00:13:55.040
Constantly.

00:13:55.040 --> 00:13:56.640
Is that would be a form of torture also?

00:13:56.640 --> 00:13:57.600
I think it is.

00:13:57.600 --> 00:14:00.720
It's weeks on end with all the lights on

00:14:00.720 --> 00:14:04.560
and hard rock blasting at you into your cell.

00:14:04.560 --> 00:14:06.560
You just can't sleep, you can't rest.

00:14:06.560 --> 00:14:11.320
And what that does is it makes a person hallucinate.

00:14:11.320 --> 00:14:14.360
In one case, we know that a prisoner lost his mind

00:14:14.360 --> 00:14:15.520
to the point where he's not able

00:14:15.520 --> 00:14:17.440
to participate in his own defense.

00:14:17.440 --> 00:14:19.560
What role does racism play in torture?

00:14:20.560 --> 00:14:22.380
Oh, I think that racism plays

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:24.160
a very large role in torture.

00:14:24.160 --> 00:14:26.320
You know, there's this famous experiment,

00:14:26.320 --> 00:14:28.440
the Stanford Prison Experiment,

00:14:28.480 --> 00:14:32.120
where a group of Stanford psychology graduate students

00:14:34.080 --> 00:14:35.400
were divided into groups.

00:14:35.400 --> 00:14:37.080
Some were gonna play the role of prisoners

00:14:37.080 --> 00:14:38.620
and some of prison guards.

00:14:39.840 --> 00:14:44.840
People started to go crazy after only 36 hours

00:14:45.080 --> 00:14:48.280
and the experiment was canceled, rather,

00:14:48.280 --> 00:14:51.000
in its entirety after only six days.

00:14:51.000 --> 00:14:54.640
And it's because people really did adopt

00:14:54.640 --> 00:14:57.600
the idea that they were better than others.

00:14:57.600 --> 00:14:59.480
We see this in American prisons every day

00:14:59.480 --> 00:15:01.400
and I can tell you that from firsthand experience.

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:02.480
Talk about that, what did you do?

00:15:02.480 --> 00:15:05.860
Sure, I spent two years in prison, 23 months in prison.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.120
I was charged with three counts of espionage,

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:13.360
one count of violating the Intelligence Identities

00:15:13.360 --> 00:15:16.280
Protection Act and one count of making a false statement

00:15:16.280 --> 00:15:19.440
because I went on ABC News in 2007

00:15:19.440 --> 00:15:22.720
and I told the whole country, the whole world,

00:15:22.720 --> 00:15:24.640
that we were torturing prisoners,

00:15:24.640 --> 00:15:27.880
that torture was official U.S. government policy

00:15:27.880 --> 00:15:30.360
and that the president had signed that policy

00:15:30.360 --> 00:15:33.560
and was lying to us when he looked into the camera

00:15:33.560 --> 00:15:37.240
and said, we do not torture, I knew that that was a lie

00:15:37.240 --> 00:15:39.680
and I said so on national news.

00:15:39.680 --> 00:15:41.680
You're very active now, you're at the Institute

00:15:41.680 --> 00:15:43.040
of Apology Study, you're really concerned

00:15:43.040 --> 00:15:45.400
about prison reform and I was wondering

00:15:45.400 --> 00:15:47.280
whether when we talk about prison reform,

00:15:47.280 --> 00:15:48.760
might we be talking about three things.

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:52.240
One, the actual lawyers themselves,

00:15:52.240 --> 00:15:54.080
to the criminal justice system

00:15:54.080 --> 00:15:56.240
and then the actual conditions in the prison.

00:15:56.240 --> 00:16:01.240
Oh yes, first of all, our entire prison system

00:16:02.600 --> 00:16:06.790
is based on racism.

00:16:06.790 --> 00:16:08.710
Look at disparities in sentencing,

00:16:08.710 --> 00:16:10.910
just as one quick example.

00:16:12.350 --> 00:16:17.350
On average, sentences for crimes related to crack

00:16:18.870 --> 00:16:21.950
are 10 times longer than sentences

00:16:21.990 --> 00:16:24.070
for crimes related to powdered cocaine.

00:16:24.070 --> 00:16:26.630
Okay, it's all cocaine, it's all the same drug,

00:16:26.630 --> 00:16:28.510
but crack is a black man's drug

00:16:28.510 --> 00:16:31.110
and powdered coke is a white man's drug

00:16:31.110 --> 00:16:34.190
and so if I'm gonna get two years

00:16:34.190 --> 00:16:36.470
because I'm selling a little bit of coke on the side,

00:16:36.470 --> 00:16:37.870
you're gonna get 20 years

00:16:37.870 --> 00:16:40.070
for selling a little bit of crack on the side.

00:16:40.070 --> 00:16:43.670
That's just inherent systemic racism.

00:16:44.710 --> 00:16:46.390
I'll give this president credit,

00:16:46.390 --> 00:16:49.870
he's trying to do something to correct those disparities

00:16:49.870 --> 00:16:52.950
but we've got an entire generation

00:16:52.950 --> 00:16:55.070
of young African-American men

00:16:55.070 --> 00:16:57.830
who's defining event in the course of their lives.

00:16:57.830 --> 00:16:58.990
Who's lives are being destroyed.

00:16:58.990 --> 00:17:02.370
Yeah, it's prison and their lives are destroyed.

00:17:02.370 --> 00:17:03.990
You do 20 years in prison

00:17:03.990 --> 00:17:05.550
for having a little bit of crack.

00:17:05.550 --> 00:17:07.110
How do you rebuild from that?

00:17:07.110 --> 00:17:10.470
Our cities aren't equipped to help people rebuild from that.

00:17:10.470 --> 00:17:11.750
And we also, within the prison,

00:17:11.750 --> 00:17:13.630
we don't hear terms like rehabilitation.

00:17:13.630 --> 00:17:14.990
Oh, never.

00:17:14.990 --> 00:17:16.630
There's no such thing as correction.

00:17:16.630 --> 00:17:18.470
There's no such thing as rehabilitation.

00:17:18.470 --> 00:17:21.870
Time was as recently as the early 1980s

00:17:21.870 --> 00:17:24.790
where you could go to prison and get a college education.

00:17:24.790 --> 00:17:26.470
You could go to prison and learn a skill.

00:17:26.470 --> 00:17:30.030
You could become a plumber or an electrician or a mechanic

00:17:30.030 --> 00:17:33.230
or any number of different things, no longer.

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:35.070
There's no money for it

00:17:35.070 --> 00:17:36.190
because we're spending the money

00:17:36.190 --> 00:17:38.550
on incarcerating these vast numbers of people.

00:17:38.550 --> 00:17:40.790
There's no money left over for education.

00:17:40.790 --> 00:17:44.550
What country has the more humane prison system?

00:17:44.550 --> 00:17:46.790
I was looking at this asset of tragedy in Norway

00:17:46.830 --> 00:17:49.710
where it seemed as if a person was treated completely different

00:17:49.710 --> 00:17:51.270
even though the crime was correct.

00:17:51.270 --> 00:17:53.230
You took the words right out of my mouth.

00:17:53.230 --> 00:17:54.990
I think Norway probably has

00:17:54.990 --> 00:17:57.670
the most progressive prison system in the world.

00:17:57.670 --> 00:18:00.350
Iceland is similarly progressive.

00:18:00.350 --> 00:18:02.190
These are all countries with no black people.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:03.030
Yeah, right.

00:18:03.030 --> 00:18:03.870
You're right.

00:18:03.870 --> 00:18:04.710
You're right.

00:18:04.710 --> 00:18:05.550
They're no people of color.

00:18:05.550 --> 00:18:09.350
Maybe the occasional Muslim immigrant and that's about it.

00:18:09.350 --> 00:18:12.510
But in those countries, when you get a sentence

00:18:12.510 --> 00:18:15.950
of life, what that means is 17 years.

00:18:15.990 --> 00:18:17.870
So at the end of 17 years, you're gonna be released.

00:18:17.870 --> 00:18:20.750
But when you're released, you've earned a college degree

00:18:20.750 --> 00:18:22.390
and you've learned a skill

00:18:22.390 --> 00:18:25.670
and you've undergone psychiatric or psychological counseling

00:18:25.670 --> 00:18:28.350
and you're ready to reintegrate into society.

00:18:28.350 --> 00:18:32.350
Here, you get nothing except a GED.

00:18:32.350 --> 00:18:36.630
They will assign another prisoner to tutor you

00:18:36.630 --> 00:18:38.430
so you can get your GED.

00:18:38.430 --> 00:18:40.550
But let's say you have your GED

00:18:40.550 --> 00:18:43.590
or you already have a high school education.

00:18:43.630 --> 00:18:46.310
You get out after doing your time

00:18:46.310 --> 00:18:48.470
and they've not taught you anything.

00:18:48.470 --> 00:18:49.750
You've earned no money.

00:18:49.750 --> 00:18:51.470
I worked a regular job.

00:18:51.470 --> 00:18:54.030
I worked every day and I made a dollar eight a month.

00:18:54.030 --> 00:18:57.430
So everybody else is making what I'm making.

00:18:57.430 --> 00:18:59.150
So you work and work and work

00:18:59.150 --> 00:19:01.030
and you're in prison for 10 years or 20 years.

00:19:01.030 --> 00:19:02.870
You're making a buck a month.

00:19:02.870 --> 00:19:05.790
You can't leave with anything to get your life started.

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:07.670
You've not learned a skill.

00:19:07.670 --> 00:19:09.190
You have no counseling.

00:19:09.190 --> 00:19:10.870
There's no job training.

00:19:10.870 --> 00:19:14.030
There's nobody helping to place you in a position.

00:19:14.030 --> 00:19:18.110
There's no mental health counseling.

00:19:18.110 --> 00:19:23.110
And so you have a lot of very angry, undereducated,

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:27.830
unemployable felons hitting the street.

00:19:27.830 --> 00:19:29.430
Well, they're gonna end up doing

00:19:29.430 --> 00:19:31.470
the only thing that they know how to do

00:19:31.470 --> 00:19:33.110
and that is to go back on the streets

00:19:33.110 --> 00:19:35.830
and sell drugs to make money to support their families.

00:19:35.830 --> 00:19:38.230
Let me mention something in terms of you

00:19:38.230 --> 00:19:39.750
responding to the prisons

00:19:39.750 --> 00:19:42.550
but also I want to bring it back to terrorism.

00:19:42.550 --> 00:19:43.750
And that's privatization.

00:19:43.750 --> 00:19:44.590
Yes.

00:19:44.590 --> 00:19:46.870
Where you look up that in some of these cases,

00:19:46.870 --> 00:19:49.270
it is not you as military people doing the torturing,

00:19:49.270 --> 00:19:51.070
but it's a private contractor.

00:19:51.070 --> 00:19:53.030
And we also went to the thing where it seems

00:19:53.030 --> 00:19:55.510
as if there was money made in terms of building prisons.

00:19:55.510 --> 00:19:58.710
I remember going out to one time to Youngstone, Ohio.

00:19:58.710 --> 00:19:59.910
One of the worst.

00:19:59.910 --> 00:20:00.870
And at the same time,

00:20:00.870 --> 00:20:02.990
if you take away that prison industry,

00:20:02.990 --> 00:20:06.070
here is a city that because of the economy,

00:20:06.070 --> 00:20:06.910
it's going bankrupt.

00:20:06.910 --> 00:20:07.750
That's all they have.

00:20:07.750 --> 00:20:08.590
That's all they have.

00:20:08.590 --> 00:20:11.510
So talk about this privatization in prison reform.

00:20:11.510 --> 00:20:13.550
I've actually, from a small town, very, very near Youngstone.

00:20:13.550 --> 00:20:16.030
You walk to Youngstone, right in a pinch.

00:20:16.030 --> 00:20:17.950
Newcastle, Pennsylvania.

00:20:17.950 --> 00:20:19.870
You're absolutely right about private prisons.

00:20:19.870 --> 00:20:24.270
This was a grave policy error that we made in the 1980s

00:20:24.270 --> 00:20:26.510
to allow prisons to be privatized.

00:20:26.510 --> 00:20:28.750
So we have private companies

00:20:28.750 --> 00:20:30.990
like the Corrections Corporation of America

00:20:30.990 --> 00:20:33.070
and they build prisons around the country,

00:20:33.070 --> 00:20:35.350
the one you mentioned being one of the most prominent

00:20:35.350 --> 00:20:37.150
in Youngstone, Ohio.

00:20:37.150 --> 00:20:40.550
But these companies, first of all,

00:20:40.550 --> 00:20:44.590
don't have an incentive to provide anything to prisoners.

00:20:44.590 --> 00:20:45.430
No education.

00:20:45.430 --> 00:20:46.670
What about the profit motor?

00:20:46.670 --> 00:20:48.030
It's all about the profits.

00:20:48.030 --> 00:20:49.710
And how do they make their profits?

00:20:49.710 --> 00:20:52.950
They're paid a certain amount of money per prisoner per year

00:20:52.950 --> 00:20:55.750
to house both federal and state prisoners.

00:20:57.350 --> 00:20:59.310
Whatever they don't spend on that prisoner,

00:20:59.310 --> 00:21:00.790
they get to keep.

00:21:00.790 --> 00:21:04.910
So they don't want to spend the money on medication

00:21:04.950 --> 00:21:09.270
or on a doctor to oversee the health of prisoners,

00:21:09.270 --> 00:21:12.630
despite the fact that diseases like tuberculosis

00:21:12.630 --> 00:21:14.550
and HIV and...

00:21:14.550 --> 00:21:16.070
And also the impact on older prisoners.

00:21:16.070 --> 00:21:18.150
Especially on older prisoners.

00:21:18.150 --> 00:21:18.990
Right.

00:21:18.990 --> 00:21:20.550
And hepatitis are rampant.

00:21:21.590 --> 00:21:23.950
And they don't want to spend the money on education

00:21:23.950 --> 00:21:25.910
or counseling or training

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:27.790
because whatever they don't spend,

00:21:27.790 --> 00:21:29.310
they can send back to their shareholders

00:21:29.310 --> 00:21:31.030
or put in their own pockets.

00:21:31.030 --> 00:21:31.870
And so...

00:21:31.870 --> 00:21:32.870
Doctor, how do you reform that?

00:21:32.870 --> 00:21:34.710
How do you break these type of cycles?

00:21:34.710 --> 00:21:36.470
Honestly, I think there are several things

00:21:36.470 --> 00:21:37.310
that have to be done.

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:38.510
First of all, I don't think we should have

00:21:38.510 --> 00:21:40.230
private prisons in this country.

00:21:40.230 --> 00:21:42.390
Nothing good comes out of a private prison.

00:21:42.390 --> 00:21:44.990
One of my cellmates, I'll just tell you a small anecdote,

00:21:44.990 --> 00:21:48.470
one of my cellmates spent just two weeks

00:21:48.470 --> 00:21:49.950
in a private prison in Virginia

00:21:49.950 --> 00:21:52.270
called the Orange County Transportation Center.

00:21:52.270 --> 00:21:57.150
220 male inmates, one toilet, the seat was broken,

00:21:57.150 --> 00:22:00.030
one TV, and there was a fist fight every hour

00:22:00.030 --> 00:22:02.910
with the winner determining what show to watch.

00:22:02.950 --> 00:22:05.150
That's inhumane.

00:22:05.150 --> 00:22:07.070
That's not the way we should be treating people

00:22:07.070 --> 00:22:07.910
in our country.

00:22:07.910 --> 00:22:09.830
But again, it's all about that profit motive.

00:22:09.830 --> 00:22:10.870
So first of all,

00:22:10.870 --> 00:22:12.470
I don't think we should have private prisons

00:22:12.470 --> 00:22:13.310
in this country.

00:22:13.310 --> 00:22:16.070
Secondly, overcrowding is absolutely criminal.

00:22:16.070 --> 00:22:19.270
And overcrowding leads to the spread of disease,

00:22:19.270 --> 00:22:21.390
it leads to violence,

00:22:21.390 --> 00:22:23.830
and it's just simply too expensive.

00:22:23.830 --> 00:22:24.990
Let me connect this to something else

00:22:24.990 --> 00:22:25.990
which we've been talking about.

00:22:25.990 --> 00:22:26.830
Sure.

00:22:26.830 --> 00:22:31.320
How does these conditions affect our national security?

00:22:31.320 --> 00:22:32.320
Wow.

00:22:32.320 --> 00:22:33.600
Well, I think,

00:22:33.600 --> 00:22:35.760
and this may be a little bit controversial,

00:22:35.760 --> 00:22:39.000
but I think that some of the policies

00:22:39.000 --> 00:22:42.760
that we have in our prisons amount to torture.

00:22:42.760 --> 00:22:46.840
For example, I think that solitary confinement

00:22:46.840 --> 00:22:48.880
is a form of torture.

00:22:48.880 --> 00:22:51.000
There's one man in particular

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:54.640
in the Angola State Penitentiary in Louisiana

00:22:54.640 --> 00:22:58.480
who's been in solitary confinement for 44 years.

00:22:58.480 --> 00:23:03.080
He has not had human interaction in 44 years.

00:23:03.080 --> 00:23:04.840
Well, a lot of these folks

00:23:04.840 --> 00:23:06.920
eventually are gonna get out.

00:23:06.920 --> 00:23:09.400
And when they get out, they're gonna be crazy

00:23:09.400 --> 00:23:11.120
and they're gonna be violent.

00:23:11.120 --> 00:23:13.440
And that destabilizes our communities.

00:23:13.440 --> 00:23:15.400
And when our communities are destabilized,

00:23:15.400 --> 00:23:16.240
the country is destabilized.

00:23:16.240 --> 00:23:17.440
Yeah, when you say that,

00:23:17.440 --> 00:23:18.800
we've gone through a number of incidents,

00:23:18.800 --> 00:23:20.320
but in terms of police brutality,

00:23:20.320 --> 00:23:21.720
when you look at a certain percentage,

00:23:21.720 --> 00:23:22.720
some of the individuals

00:23:22.720 --> 00:23:24.520
have suffered some form of mental illness.

00:23:24.520 --> 00:23:25.360
Absolutely.

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:26.800
And I think that's the thing in terms of,

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:27.880
you arrive on a scene

00:23:27.880 --> 00:23:29.840
and someone's acting in a certain way and they're black.

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:30.680
Well, what happens,

00:23:30.680 --> 00:23:32.800
no one knows whether that person lack medication,

00:23:32.840 --> 00:23:35.240
counseling or whatever, you just see the result.

00:23:35.240 --> 00:23:37.160
Yeah, and what happens more often than not,

00:23:37.160 --> 00:23:39.480
unfortunately, is the police just open fire.

00:23:39.480 --> 00:23:40.320
Right.

00:23:40.320 --> 00:23:42.480
Let me ask you this when you're getting in our show.

00:23:42.480 --> 00:23:44.760
Using your skills as an analyst,

00:23:44.760 --> 00:23:46.280
what human rights and private issues

00:23:46.280 --> 00:23:48.680
should we be concerned about in the next 10 years?

00:23:51.240 --> 00:23:53.400
Well, we could write a book about it.

00:23:53.400 --> 00:23:55.720
I'll come back for another program.

00:23:55.720 --> 00:24:00.000
Privacy is absolutely critically important right now

00:24:00.000 --> 00:24:01.360
to the American people.

00:24:01.360 --> 00:24:05.040
And it's because we've essentially lost our privacy

00:24:05.040 --> 00:24:07.240
without even so much as a debate.

00:24:07.240 --> 00:24:08.080
We really had to.

00:24:08.080 --> 00:24:08.920
Let me ask you this.

00:24:08.920 --> 00:24:09.920
You work for the CIA.

00:24:09.920 --> 00:24:11.240
Hey, I mean,

00:24:11.240 --> 00:24:13.080
weren't you concerned about people's privacy?

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:14.160
I mean, you know.

00:24:14.160 --> 00:24:17.560
Well, the CIA is focused on foreign intelligence collectively.

00:24:17.560 --> 00:24:21.440
And since the reforms of the church committee in 1975,

00:24:21.440 --> 00:24:24.920
76, I wasn't concerned.

00:24:24.920 --> 00:24:25.760
I am now.

00:24:25.760 --> 00:24:28.080
I'm talking about privacy of people overseas though.

00:24:28.080 --> 00:24:29.760
Well, you're right.

00:24:29.800 --> 00:24:32.080
I want to spy on people overseas.

00:24:32.080 --> 00:24:35.360
Don't forget, you know, and I say this all the time,

00:24:35.360 --> 00:24:37.960
that progressives are patriots too.

00:24:37.960 --> 00:24:40.200
And I wanted to do everything I could

00:24:40.200 --> 00:24:41.920
to protect the American people.

00:24:41.920 --> 00:24:44.200
So I want to bring you back to a question I asked you earlier.

00:24:44.200 --> 00:24:45.680
These philosophical ethical issues.

00:24:45.680 --> 00:24:46.520
Yes.

00:24:46.520 --> 00:24:48.760
It's not like gravity is different over there than here.

00:24:48.760 --> 00:24:50.400
I mean, spying is spying, isn't it?

00:24:50.400 --> 00:24:51.880
Yes, spying is spying.

00:24:51.880 --> 00:24:56.000
And certainly, most of our allies spy on us

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:57.240
just like we spy on them.

00:24:57.240 --> 00:24:59.040
So what do you see as being a key issue

00:24:59.040 --> 00:25:00.640
in the next couple of years, in turn?

00:25:00.640 --> 00:25:04.160
I think the key issue is our intelligence community

00:25:04.160 --> 00:25:06.080
organization is spying on us.

00:25:06.080 --> 00:25:07.800
Spying on American citizens.

00:25:07.800 --> 00:25:09.520
For example, NSA.

00:25:09.520 --> 00:25:13.120
Not only is it illegal for NSA to spy on American citizens,

00:25:13.120 --> 00:25:18.720
but it's in NSA's charter from its creation

00:25:18.720 --> 00:25:21.520
that it's not permitted to spy on American citizens.

00:25:21.520 --> 00:25:23.580
Well, we would have had no idea

00:25:23.580 --> 00:25:26.800
that NSA was spying on us had Ed Snowden not

00:25:26.800 --> 00:25:28.120
gone public with his revelations.

00:25:28.120 --> 00:25:29.280
How do you view Snowden?

00:25:29.280 --> 00:25:31.080
I think Ed Snowden's a national hero.

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:33.080
I really do.

00:25:33.080 --> 00:25:36.080
He is the very definition of a whistleblower, Ethelbert.

00:25:36.080 --> 00:25:40.600
He revealed evidence of waste, fraud, abuse, or illegality.

00:25:40.600 --> 00:25:44.680
And to me, that makes him a hero, not a criminal.

00:25:44.680 --> 00:25:47.640
And when we look at how a person should

00:25:47.640 --> 00:25:49.160
read your book, The Reluctant Spy,

00:25:49.160 --> 00:25:54.040
should we read it reluctantly?

00:25:54.040 --> 00:25:58.600
I call myself a reluctant spy for a couple of reasons.

00:25:58.600 --> 00:26:00.680
One, I enjoyed analysis very much.

00:26:00.680 --> 00:26:02.840
I was reluctant to get into operations.

00:26:02.840 --> 00:26:06.400
But I did, and I enjoyed it, and I was good at it.

00:26:06.400 --> 00:26:10.360
I was reluctant to allow my marriage

00:26:10.360 --> 00:26:12.400
to break up because of my CIA work.

00:26:12.400 --> 00:26:14.080
And that's exactly what happened.

00:26:14.080 --> 00:26:16.960
I go into some detail about that.

00:26:16.960 --> 00:26:22.280
And I was reluctant to the point of being unwilling

00:26:22.280 --> 00:26:28.280
to engage in what I believed to be war crimes or crimes

00:26:28.280 --> 00:26:32.800
against humanity when the CIA began violating

00:26:32.800 --> 00:26:35.560
the human rights of those that we were capturing.

00:26:35.560 --> 00:26:38.040
Well, John, I'm glad I captured you for the show,

00:26:38.040 --> 00:26:39.400
and I'll let you go.

00:26:39.400 --> 00:26:41.840
But I want to thank you in terms of being my guest here.

00:26:41.840 --> 00:26:43.760
Thanks for joining me on The Scholars.

00:26:43.760 --> 00:26:45.000
Thank you for watching.

00:26:45.000 --> 00:26:47.040
I'm Ethelbert Miller.

